    "result_text": "The test suite is green. Here's what I found and verified.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification is fully satisfied by the existing exception-handling layer \u2014 all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) already return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body containing `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, with the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\n**How the rejections flow to problem+json:**\n\nAll rejections funnel through `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, which builds a `ProblemDetail` (`detailBuild`) that sets:\n- `type` (a URI, from the request URL)\n- `title` (the exception's simple name)\n- `status` (via `ProblemDetail.forStatus`)\n- `detail` (the human-readable message)\n\nThe two rejection paths:\n- **400 (bean-validation)** \u2192 `handleMethodArgumentNotValidException`\n- **400 / 409 / 429 (business rules in `OwnerRestControllerV1`, e.g. postcode/address validation, duplicate identity, city capacity, daily create limit)** \u2192 thrown as `ResponseStatusException` \u2192 `handleResponseStatusException`\n\nBoth return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`; Spring Framework auto-negotiates the `application/problem+json` content type for `ProblemDetail` bodies.\n\n**Verification performed:**\n- The new test `AcceptanceTest#coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (400) passes \u2014 confirms exact content type `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`.\n- I added temporary tests exercising the 409 (duplicate identity) and 429 (daily create limit) paths, asserting the content type plus presence of `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail`. Both passed, confirming the `ResponseStatusException` handler also emits compliant problem+json. I then removed those temporary tests.\n- Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test run, 0 failures.\n\nNo source changes were required \u2014 the codebase already implements the specified behavior, and the suite is green.",
